# Code Style & Quality

## Standard & flags

- **C11** (`-std=c11`)
- `-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`
- `-D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L`
- Zero warnings — all treated as errors

## Formatting

- **Indentation:** 4 spaces. No tabs.
- **Function definitions:** opening brace on its own line.
- **Line length:** prefer ≤80 characters where practical.
- **Files end with a single newline.**

## Module conventions

- **`src/main.c`** stays thin — wiring only; flag parsing lives in `rm_toto_cli.c`, removal logic lives in `rm_toto_walk.c`.
- **`include/rm_toto.h`** — `static inline rm_toto_join_path()` is intentional (shared by `rm_toto_walk.c` and tests without linking `src/`).
- **One concern per `src/rm_toto_*.c`** — do not collapse back into a monolith without reason.
- **Platform code:** use `#if defined(_WIN32)` / `#else` only where MSYS2's POSIX emulation genuinely diverges (e.g. documenting why open-file removal may fail); do not add Windows-specific unlink retry logic unless explicitly requested — that is scope creep on top of `rm`'s contract.

## Naming

| Kind | Style | Examples |
|------|-------|----------|
| Functions | `snake_case` | `rm_toto_remove_path`, `rm_toto_join_path`, `rm_toto_confirm` |
| Macros / constants | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `RM_TOTO_PATH_MAX`, `RM_TOTO_EXIT_FAILURE` |
| Headers | `RM_TOTO_*_H` guards | `RM_TOTO_H`, `RM_TOTO_WALK_H` |

Public API prefix: **`rm_toto_`** for module entry points; `rm_toto_join_path` is the spec name for the inline helper.

## Memory

- **No heap anywhere in-tree** — every path fits a stack `char path_buf[RM_TOTO_PATH_MAX]`; no `malloc`, no `realloc`, no heap fallback (contrast with `yes-toto`'s justified pre-loop `malloc`, which does not apply here).
- Recursion uses one stack path buffer per frame, not a shared/global buffer.

## Recursive walk rules (`rm_toto_walk.c`)

- `lstat()` every entry before deciding how to handle it — **never** `stat()`; symlinks are unlinked, never dereferenced.
- `unlink()` for files/symlinks/other non-directory types; `rmdir()` for directories, only after all children are gone (post-order).
- No `remove()` — use explicit `unlink()`/`rmdir()` per project convention.
- Errors on one entry are reported and traversal continues with siblings; no `exit()` mid-tree.
- No `printf` / `fprintf` / `puts` / `fputs` / `fwrite` for `-v` output inside the walk — raw `write(2)` via `rm_toto_emit_verbose()`.

## Errors

- **stderr only:** `rm-toto: <context>: <strerror(errno)>`
- Implemented in `rm_toto_emit.c`
- Confirmation prompts also go to stderr; `-v` output goes to stdout.

## Comments

- Chain-of-thought block at top of each `src/*.c` and test file (see existing files).
- Contract comments on non-trivial functions: preconditions, postconditions, return value, error behaviour.
- Comment non-obvious decisions (path buffer sizing, post-order rationale, symlink handling); not obvious code.

## Tests

- Plain `assert()` — no external test framework.
- One scenario per `tests/test_*.c`; runner in `tests/test_runner.c`.
- Print `PASS: <name>` on success.
- No filesystem I/O in tests — only pure helpers (`rm_toto_join_path`, `rm_toto_is_root_path`) are unit-tested; see `testing.mdc`.

## What not to do

- Do not compile as C++.
- Do not link libraries beyond libc.
- Do not use `remove()`, unbounded string copies, or ignore `unlink()`/`rmdir()` return values.
- Do not follow symlinks when walking a tree.
- Do not add `-P` (secure overwrite) or any trash/recycle-bin behaviour unless explicitly requested.
- Do not install a custom `SIGINT` handler — default disposition is correct for this project.
